WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.400
A jar of syrup can look trustworthy while concealing an

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.864
unstable preparation. That difficulty was familiar to

00:00:08.864 --> 00:00:11.339
pharmacists long before industrial manufacturing.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.709
A prescription was not finished when its ingredients

00:00:19.709 --> 00:00:23.375
were combined. In medieval Cairo, a medicine still had

00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:25.004
to be made reliable.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.980
The Minhaj al-dukkan associated with al-Kuhin al-Attar

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.510
documents practical pharmaceutical choices. It is an

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.041
historical text, not a modern clinical trial.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.852
Concentration, ingredients and preservation are

00:00:48.852 --> 00:00:53.176
questions of practice. A formula means little if the

00:00:53.176 --> 00:00:56.539
preparation changes before it reaches its user.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.661
A pharmacist with an unavailable ingredient had to

00:01:05.661 --> 00:01:08.819
consider replacement. Similar names or therapeutic

00:01:08.819 --> 00:01:11.977
intentions did not guarantee material equivalence.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.583
Recipes described heat, consistency and substitutions.

00:01:19.583 --> 00:01:23.238
The Cambridge Genizah prescription offers a separate

00:01:23.238 --> 00:01:27.415
documentary glimpse, not a full account of practice.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.003
Formulary instructions and documentary traces allow an

00:01:35.003 --> 00:01:38.048
investigation of pharmaceutical reliability, but they

00:01:38.048 --> 00:01:42.617
do not establish the clinical success of each recipe.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.424
The historical evidence shows attempts to make medicine

00:01:51.424 --> 00:01:54.902
dependable. It does not justify calling medieval

00:01:54.902 --> 00:01:56.889
practice modern quality assurance.
